<ArticleLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} ogImage={ogImage} heroImage={heroImage} heroAlt={heroAlt} schema={schema} publishDate={publishDate} readTime={readTime} category="PNR Guide">
  <section>
    <p>Facing the screen of a Global Distribution System (GDS) for the first time can be overwhelming. Lines of green text on a black background, filled with letters, numbers, and acronyms that seem to make no sense. For travel agents, mastering this language is an absolute requirement to operate efficiently. Understanding how to read a PNR (Passenger Name Record) allows you to manage reservations, check flight statuses, and ensure your clients have their itinerary in perfect order.</p>

    <p>Learning the technical structure behind these reservations will give you operational confidence. In this article, we will break down every element of an Amadeus-generated PNR so you can quickly identify dates, flights, classes, and time limits.</p>

    <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6 my-6">
      <a href="/en/pricing/" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-6 py-3 bg-magenta hover:bg-magenta-500 text-white font-semibold rounded-full transition-colors">Start your 7-day free trial. No credit card. Setup in 24h.</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The Base Structure: The PRINT System</h2>
    <p>Any reservation created in the Amadeus terminal follows a highly structured logic. As we reviewed in our guide on <a href="/en/blog/what-is-pnr/">what a PNR is</a>, the mandatory information is organized under the acronym PRINT. To read the file correctly, you must know how to identify where each of these elements is located within the text block.</p>

    <p>When you retrieve a reservation using the locator code (example: <code>RT ABCDEF</code>), the screen displays a numbered list. Each line number corresponds to a segment of information (name, flight, phone, etc.). Let's analyze the core lines.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Breakdown: How to Read the Itinerary Line (Flights)</h2>
    <p>The itinerary segment is usually the longest and most complex part. A reading error here will likely result in a passenger missing a flight. Let's look at a classic example of a flight from New York to London with British Airways:</p>

    <pre><code>2. BA 0112 Y 15NOV JFK LHR HK1 1830 0630+1</code></pre>

    <p>This line is read from left to right, dividing it into the following blocks:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>2.</strong> - This is the line number within the PNR.</li>
      <li><strong>BA</strong> - The IATA code for the airline (British Airways).</li>
      <li><strong>0112</strong> - The specific flight number.</li>
      <li><strong>Y</strong> - The booking class (Full economy class in this case).</li>
      <li><strong>15NOV</strong> - The departure date (November 15th).</li>
      <li><strong>JFK LHR</strong> - Origin airport (John F. Kennedy, New York) and destination airport (Heathrow, London).</li>
      <li><strong>HK1</strong> - Status code and number of passengers. "HK" stands for <em>Holds Confirmed</em>, and "1" indicates there is one reserved seat.</li>
      <li><strong>1830 0630+1</strong> - Departure time (18:30) and arrival time (06:30). The "+1" suggests that the flight lands the day after the departure date.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Understanding this structure is vital. If instead of "HK" you saw codes like "HL" (Waitlist) or "UN" (Flight cancelled), you would immediately know you need to take action to re-accommodate your client.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Identifying Names, Phones, and Time Limits</h2>
    <p>In addition to flights, the system records traveler data using specific formats that you must learn to scan visually.</p>

    <h3>The Name Field</h3>
    <p>It usually appears on the first line of the PNR. The standard format in Amadeus is <code>1.SURNAME/FIRSTNAME MR</code>. If several people are traveling, you will see <code>1.SMITH/JOHN MR 2.SMITH/MARY MRS</code>. The initial number associates that passenger with the rest of the assigned services in the itinerary.</p>

    <h3>The Ticketing Field</h3>
    <p>The time limit is critical data that defines when the reservation will be cancelled if the ticket is not issued. You will find it with the format <code>TK TL</code> (Ticketing Time Limit) followed by the date. Example: <code>TK TL 10NOV/1200</code>. This indicates you have until November 10 at 12:00 PM to process the payment; otherwise, the seats will return to the airline's inventory.</p>

    <div class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-xl p-6 my-6">
      <a href="/" class="inline-flex items-center justify-center px-6 py-3 border border-slate-300 text-slate-700 hover:border-magenta hover:text-magenta font-semibold rounded-full transition-colors">Ready to quote like agencies that sell seriously? Try Quotix free for 7 days.</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The Problem with Client Communication</h2>
    <p>Knowing how to read this turns you into an operational expert. However, sending this cryptic code to a passenger creates severe commercial friction. The client doesn't know that "HK" means confirmed, nor do they understand that "LHR" is Heathrow. When a travel agent copies and pastes this text into an email or WhatsApp, they force the user to decode the information on their own.</p>

    <p>Furthermore, there is a serious privacy risk. PNRs often have lines labeled <code>RM</code> (Remarks) or <code>FM</code> (Commission), where the agency notes down net costs, profitability margins, or private agreements with suppliers. Sharing the entire text block could expose your commercial data.</p>
  </section>

